etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4171676\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: thenationalnews.com\nSLUG: deterrence-and-leverage-iran-tries-to-call-the-shots-with-strikes-on-israel\nTEXT: Iran\'s recent strikes on Israel have sent a clear message that it will not back down in the face of threats, with Chairman of Iran\'s Expediency Council Sadeq Larijani stating that any assault on the resistance axis would be met with a response "transcending geographical borders". This proactive initiative aims to safeguard regional power, rather than reacting to threats. In response, Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu has stated that Israel "will not accept" a situation where it is attacked every time Beirut\'s southern suburbs are targeted. The exchange of ballistic missiles and air strikes continued, with Israel launching new attacks on cities including Tehran and Isfahan.\n\nID: 4172375\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2360653\nTEXT: Israel struck Iran, defying Trump\'s call for restraint. Tehran insists any deal to end the war must also halt the conflict in Lebanon, where Israel is pursuing a campaign against Hezbollah. Iran\'s Revolutionary Guards called the attack a "warning" after Israel struck Beirut\'s southern suburbs, threatening wider strikes if repeated. A separate Iranian attack targeting "terrorist groups" in Iraqi Kurdistan added strain to hopes for a lasting peace.\n\nID: 4172764\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: ctvnews.ca\nSLUG: israel-and-iran-appear-to-pause-strikes-after-trading-fire-for-the-first-time-since-april-ceasefire\nTEXT: Israel and Iran appear to have paused their strikes, following a rare exchange of fire since the April ceasefire. Maj.-Gen. (Ret\'d) Denis Thompson, a Canadian military expert, notes that these are the first ballistic missiles launched by Iran since the April ceasefire. The recent escalation in tensions has raised concerns about a potential return to conflict, but for now, it seems that both sides have decided to hold their fire.\n\nID: 4172815\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: scmp.com\nSLUG: israel-iran-appear-pause-strikes-after-trading-fire-first-time-ceasefire\nTEXT: Israel and Iran appeared to pause their strikes after trading fire for the first time since a ceasefire was agreed upon by the US two months ago. The US had brokered a ceasefire, which led to a brief exchange of fire between the two nations. Iran warned that further aggression by Israel, including in southern Lebanon, would be met with "much more severe" measures.\n\nID: 4171885\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: thecitizen.co.tz\nSLUG: israel-iran-strike-each-other-for-first-time-since-ceasefire-5489048\nTEXT: Israel and Iran have clashed for the first time since a ceasefire, with both countries targeting strategic infrastructure. The confrontation marks a serious breakdown in the fragile ceasefire regime, as Israel and Iran now openly target energy and industrial sites. This pattern could further destabilize the region, disrupting global energy markets.\n\nID: 4171992\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: themoscowtimes.com\nSLUG: russia-tells-citizens-to-avoid-traveling-to-israel-after-iranian-attacks-a92955\nTEXT: Russia has advised its citizens to avoid traveling to Israel amid escalating tensions following Iranian attacks on Israeli targets. Iran\'s military has vowed to continue its strikes against Israel, warning of "much more severe and crushing measures" if aggression continues. In response, Israel has halted its attacks on Iran.\n\n\n',)
