etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4171063\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: thestar.com.my\nSLUG: mindanao-hit-by-fifth-in-a-series-of-quakes\nTEXT: A fifth earthquake with a moderate magnitude of 5.3 struck Mindanao, Philippines on Monday (June 8) at 1:12pm. The tremor occurred at a depth of 70km, approximately 155km north-west of the Talaud Islands. Similar to previous quakes, no tsunami threat has been issued for Malaysia. The earthquake was followed by another strong quake at 7:37am with a magnitude of 7.9, and two more quakes with magnitudes of 6.5 and 5.6 at depths of 20km and 44km, respectively. Tsunami warning sirens were activated along several coastal areas in Sabah, including Tawau and Semporna, before being officially lifted at noon.\n\nID: 4171070\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2360764\nTEXT: The Philippine Institute of Volcanology and Seismology (Phivolcs) director Teresito Bacolcol suggests that the recent deadly quake may be attributed to subduction along the Cotabato Trench, a highly active fault system capable of generating massive earthquakes and tsunamis. The quake\'s offshore epicenter was 32 kilometers west of Sarangani\'s Maasim municipality, and its impact was felt as far as the coastal municipalities of Abuyog and Dulagin in Leyte. As of 11 a.m., Phivolcs recorded 138 aftershocks, with magnitudes ranging from 1.3 to 6.7.\n\nID: 4170675\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: globaltimes.cn\nSLUG: 1363039-shtml\nTEXT: A 7:37 a.m. local time tectonic earthquake occurred at a depth of 33 km, with the epicenter 32 km southwest of Maasim town in Sarangani province on Mindanao Island. The Philippine National Police reported 134 injuries, while the Philippine Institute of Volcanology and Seismology documented the earthquake\'s location.\n\n\n',)
