etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4171150\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: indiatoday.in\nSLUG: sensex-nifty-crude-oil-markets-hit-two-month-lows-as-iran-israel-tensions-crude-price-rise-2923554-2026-06-08\nTEXT: The Sensex and Nifty indices plummeted to two-month lows due to rising crude oil prices, exacerbated by tensions between Iran and Israel. The Middle East conflict overshadowed other market triggers, with oil prices surging sharply higher. This has significant implications for India, where high crude oil prices can fuel inflation, pressure the rupee, and weigh on economic growth. The Reserve Bank of India\'s efforts to control inflation may be complicated if crude oil prices reach the $100-per-barrel mark. Additionally, a global technology selloff has added to market pressure, with the Nifty IT index falling 1.23%. Stocks such as TCS, Infosys, and HCL Technologies also declined, while the Nifty Realty index fell 2.56% and India VIX jumped 7.85%.\n\nID: 4172311\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2361009\nTEXT: The Philippine Stock Exchange index (PSEi) fell 0.99 percent, or 58.97 points, to close at 5,879.41 on Monday. The broader All Shares index also ended lower. The peso weakened by 22 centavos to P61.69:$1, due to the dollar\'s strengthening amid expectations of a Federal Reserve rate hike and renewed conflict in the Middle East. Global oil prices rose, causing the local currency to depreciate within the day. Investors adopted a defensive stance as geopolitical risks intensified, with selling pressure mirroring sharp declines across regional markets.\n\nID: 4170792\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: news18.com\nSLUG: indian-stock-market-climbs-back-above-taiwan-south-korea-as-tech-stocks-tumble-ws-l-10136130-html\nTEXT: The Indian stock market has regained its position as the 5th largest market in Asia, surpassing Taiwan and South Korea after a tech-led sell-off hit Samsung Electronics, SK Hynix, and TSMC. As of noon on June 8, India\'s market capitalization stood at $4.36 trillion, ahead of Taiwan ($4.33 trillion) and South Korea ($4.33 trillion). The tech sell-off in Taiwan and South Korea led to a sharp decline in major chip stocks, with Samsung Electronics falling as much as 11% intraday.\n\nID: 4172306\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2361013\nTEXT: Moody\'s warns that a stronger US dollar poses credit quality risks for some Philippine companies with structural currency mismatches. The report notes that the Indonesian rupiah, Indian rupee, and Philippine peso have depreciated by 10-12% amid war in the Middle East, higher oil prices, and US tariffs. Companies with substantial dollar liabilities but local-currency revenues are most vulnerable to currency depreciation, which reflects differences in revenue and cost currency profiles.\n\n\n',)
