etadata field is not consistent, set it to null.\n   - Metadata keys:\n       - location\n       - subject_matter\n       - industry\n       - time  (represents WHEN the event occurred, not when published)\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object with this structure:\n\n{\n  "subject": "...",\n  "predicate": "...",\n  "object": "...",\n  "metadata": {\n    "location": "... or null",\n    "subject_matter": "... or null",\n    "industry": "... or null",\n    "time": "... or null"\n  }\n}\n\nHere are the RawRecords:\n\nID: 4171736\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: emirates247.com\nSLUG: 2356\nTEXT: At least 32 people are feared dead and dozens injured after a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck off the southern Philippine island of Mindanao on Monday, with strong tremors felt across the region. The quake hit early in the morning around 20 km off Sarangani province, causing widespread damage and landslides. Civil defence officials are verifying reports of 32 fatalities and at least 134 people injured, with the hardest-hit areas including General Santos City. Emergency response teams have been deployed to prepare relief supplies and evacuation centres, with President Ferdinand Marcos Jr ordering an immediate response. Damage is significant, with buildings and shops reduced to rubble in General Santos City, and a hospital evacuated due to structural concerns.\n\nID: 4171790\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: scrippsnews.com\nSLUG: more-than-30-dead-after-7-8-magnitude-quake-hits-in-the-philippines\nTEXT: At least 30 people are dead after a 7.8 magnitude earthquake hit the Philippines, with more than 12 remaining missing in General Santos. The quake was centered at sea off Mindanao, causing widespread damage and destruction. Search and rescue teams are scrambling to find people trapped in buildings, including a supermarket, warehouse, grade school, and other small structures. The international airport was temporarily shut due to the earthquake, with 17 domestic flights canceled. Philippine President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. ordered classes to be cancelled and disaster-response agencies to work immediately on quake-hit provinces. The Pacific Tsunami Warning Center said the threat of a tsunami largely passed, but waves up to 3 feet were monitored in some provinces.\n\nID: 4171052\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: gulftoday.ae\nSLUG: major-earthquake-off-philippines-kills-at-least-15\nTEXT: A major earthquake off the Philippines has killed at least 15 people. Japanese authorities issued a tsunami advisory for parts of its Pacific coast, but waves reaching the country\'s coast were reported to be no larger than 20 centimeters. The Philippines is prone to earthquakes due to its location on the Pacific "Ring of Fire". In October, a pair of earthquakes with magnitudes 7.4 and 6.7 hit Eastern Mindanao, killing at least eight people. A magnitude 6.9 quake days earlier killed 76 people and destroyed or damaged 72,000 buildings in Cebu province.\n\nID: 4170447\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: asiaone.com\nSLUG: 15-feared-killed-after-78-magnitude-earthquake-strikes-southern-philippines\nTEXT: At least 15 people are feared dead after a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck off the island of Mindanao in southern Philippines on June 8, triggering tsunami warnings across several countries. The epicentre of the quake was located about 20 km off Mindanao\'s Sarangani province. Schools were reopening in the Philippines after a long break, with the tremors felt strongly in a dozen provinces and 420 km away in the city of Manado on the Indonesian island of Sulawesi.\n\nID: 4171561\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: scmp.com\nSLUG: mindanao-quake-exposes-philippines-vulnerability-big-one-tremor-metro-manila\nTEXT: A magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck southern Mindanao on Monday morning, exposing the Philippines\' vulnerability to a "Big One" tremor in Metro Manila. The powerful quake triggered tsunami warnings along the southern coast, leaving collapsed buildings, damaged homes and debris-strewn streets in its wake. Disaster planners warn that limited evacuation routes in densely populated Metro Manila would make rapid, orderly evacuation a major challenge.\n\nID: 4171570\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: asiaone.com\nSLUG: philippines-earthquake-mindanao-death-toll-damage\nTEXT: The death toll in a 7.8-magnitude earthquake off the southern Philippine island of Mindanao has risen to at least 32, with dozens injured. The quake triggered tsunami warnings across several countries and was felt strongly across Mindanao, 420km away in Manado on Sulawesi. Preliminary reports indicate 32 people killed and 134 injured, mostly from falling debris and landslides.\n\nID: 4170493\nCREATED: 2026-06-08\nDOMAIN: manilatimes.net\nSLUG: 2360331\nTEXT: A 7.4-magnitude earthquake struck off the southern Philippines, triggering tsunami warnings and killing at least three people. Japanese authorities issued a tsunami advisory for the Pacific coast, predicting waves of up to 1 meter (3 feet) in different regions. The Philippines is prone to earthquakes due to its location on the Pacific "Ring of Fire".\n\n\n',)
